UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 33, Lesson 1: Stagecraft: sets, lighting and sound', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Stagecraft supports story through set, lighting ideas and sound cues. In classrooms, sets can be modular tables/cloths; lighting can be torch angles or open blinds; sound can be live or device cues. Every design choice should serve meaning, not clutter.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPropose set, light and sound for a library-after-hours mystery scene.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Links each design choice to mood/story, keeps ideas classroom-safe.\n\nWATCH FOR\nPiling props because they look cool. Select for story.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCreate a stagecraft plan sheet for one scene with labelled set/light/sound and the story reason for each.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Stagecraft supports story through set, lighting ideas and sound cues. In classrooms, sets can be modular tables/cloths; lighting can be torch angles or open blinds; sound can be live or device cues. Every design choice should serve meaning, not clutter.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Create a stagecraft plan sheet for one scene with labelled set/light/sound and the story reason for each. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using stagecraft on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Piling props because they look cool.. Correction: Strong answer: Links each design choice to mood/story, keeps ideas classroom-safe.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Piling props because they look cool. Select for story. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating stagecraft on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with stagecraft, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about stagecraft with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for stagecraft. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for stagecraft. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson.","Links each design choice to mood/story, keeps ideas classroom-safe.","Piling props because they look cool.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material."],"question":"Propose set, light and sound for a library-after-hours mystery scene. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for stagecraft; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Links each design choice to mood/story, keeps ideas classroom-safe.","Careful use of set and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Piling props because they look cool.","Stagecraft supports story through set, lighting ideas and sound cues."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with stagecraft?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named set and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name set and explain why it matters only, without linking it to stagecraft.","Apply stagecraft to new material and name the set and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Piling props because they look cool."],"question":"Which response best applies stagecraft with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on stagecraft. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Piling props because they look cool.","Links each design choice to mood/story, keeps ideas classroom-safe."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for stagecraft?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for stagecraft.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Piling props because they look cool."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about stagecraft?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example: For a storm scene, grey cloth backdrop, torch flickered upward for lightning, drum cue for thunder.","Piling props because they look cool.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Stagecraft supports story through set, lighting ideas and sound cues. — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for stagecraft?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name set and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","Create a stagecraft plan sheet for one scene with labelled set/light/sound and the story reason for each.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Piling props because they look cool."],"question":"Which independent response best applies stagecraft to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about stagecraft with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating stagecraft on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using stagecraft on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for stagecraft. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with stagecraft, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Piling props because they look cool.. Correction: Strong answer: Links each design choice to mood/story, keeps ideas classroom-safe.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y4-w33-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise stagecraft.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use stagecraft with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify set and explain why it matters.","I can practise lighting idea independently on a new prompt.","I can notice sound cue needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Stagecraft supports story through set, lighting ideas and sound cues.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Stagecraft supports story through set, lighting ideas and sound cues."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about stagecraft with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: For a storm scene, grey cloth backdrop, torch flickered upward for lightning, drum cue for thunder. Audience reads ''storm'' without explanation.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPropose set, light and sound for a library-after-hours mystery scene.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Links each design choice to mood/story, keeps ideas classroom-safe.","explain":"Stagecraft supports story through set, lighting ideas and sound cues. In classrooms, sets can be modular tables/cloths; lighting can be torch angles or open blinds; sound can be live or device cues. Every design choice should serve meaning, not clutter.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Piling props because they look cool. Select for story. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Stagecraft tools.","caption":"Serve the story.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill